      "CustomerSummary": "Customer A, a woman in her early sixties with an income of $50,000 and a liquid net worth of $700,000, was subject to excessive trading by Robert Gleason between July 2020 and June 2021. Gleason executed 91 trades in Customer A's account, resulting in a cost-to-equity ratio exceeding 28% and a turnover rate of 12.93. With an average account balance of approximately $101,000, Gleason purchased approximately $1.3 million in securities, generating more than $28,000 in commissions and trade costs. This pattern of excessive trading constituted a Care Obligation violation under Reg BI because: (1) the turnover rate of 12.93 was more than double the threshold of 6 that generally indicates excessive trading; (2) the cost-to-equity ratio of 28% exceeded the 20% threshold that generally indicates excessive trading; and (3) Gleason engaged in a pattern of in-and-out, short-term trading without considering the cumulative costs to the customer. Gleason later settled with Customer A through voluntary mediation.",
      "OverallSummary": "This case involves a Care Obligation violation by Robert Spaulding Gleason Jr. under Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI). Between July 2020 and June 2021, while associated with Cantella & Co., Inc., Gleason willfully violated Exchange Act Rule 15l-1(a)(1) by recommending a series of excessive transactions in the account of an elderly retail customer (Customer A). The excessive trading was established through the application of specific quantitative tests: (1) a turnover rate of 12.93, which is more than double the threshold of 6 that generally indicates excessive trading; and (2) a cost-to-equity ratio exceeding 28%, which is significantly above the 20% threshold that generally indicates excessive trading. Gleason executed 91 trades in Customer A's account over 11 months. Although Customer A's average account balance during this period was approximately $101,000, Gleason effected the purchase of approximately $1.3 million in securities, generating more than $28,000 in commissions and trade costs. The customer would have needed her account to grow by more than 28% during the 11-month period just to break even. Gleason engaged in a pattern of in-and-out, short-term trading without considering the cumulative costs to the customer. As a result of these violations, Gleason was fined $5,000 and suspended for three months. The case is notable as it demonstrates FINRA's enforcement of Reg BI's Care Obligation, which requires brokers to ensure that a series of recommended transactions is not excessive and is in the retail customer's best interest in light of their investment profile."
